Feature: Whether a runtime class can host a sandbox
  OpenShell's sandbox runtime confines its agent inside the workload pod with Landlock, seccomp user notification and reads of the task's memory, and fails closed without them; a sandbox also needs the cluster's resolver, its Services and the pod network's MTU. just runtime-check puts the question to the cluster for one runtime class, kata, gvisor or none, the default runtime: it runs OpenShell's own capability probe from its pinned sandbox image in a pod shaped like OpenShell's workload, and a second pod that looks a cluster name up, connects to the resolver's Service address and reads its default route's MTU, compared with an ordinary pod's on the same node. It prints pass or FAIL with the reason for each of kernel, name, service and mtu, judges no class, and leaves nothing on the cluster.

  Scenario: The runtime check reports OpenShell's capability probe, a cluster name, a Service connection and the default route's MTU for the class it is given, and passes when all four pass
    Given a copy of the scripts with a kubeconfig of its own and a recording kubectl that keeps the namespace and the pods as state, schedules each pod, and answers each pod's end and log from canned files: the probe's qualified report, and the in-pod script's lines with every check good and equal route MTUs
    When just runtime-check runs for kata, for gvisor and for none, and the in-pod script the recipe sends is run with stand-ins for its tools
    Then it prints one line naming the class, or the default runtime, the node, the kernel release, the LSMs and how many seconds after the pod was created its first output came, then pass for kernel with the Landlock ABI, seccomp notification with addfd send and the task-memory copy the report names, for name, for service and for mtu with both MTUs and where each came from, in that order, and exits 0; net and probe run under the class and reference under the default runtime, probe and reference on net's node; and the in-pod script prints its key=value lines, the MTU from the route or the device, and no address

  Scenario: The runtime check fails, naming each check that fails and why, when the probe fails, a name does not resolve, a Service does not answer or the MTU differs
    Given the same copy, whose recording kubectl answers with a probe that exits 1 with OpenShell's diagnostic, one killed with nothing printed, one that exits 1 after a qualified report, one that exits 0 with a report that is not qualified or lacks what the pass names, net's lines saying the name did not resolve and the connection timed out, lines missing or malformed, an MTU that differs, that net or the reference cannot read, or whose devices agree while the routes differ, a reference that did not run, and a net killed after it printed every line
    When just runtime-check runs in each case, and the in-pod script runs with stand-ins whose lookup finds nothing, takes too long or answers without an IPv4 address, whose connection times out or is refused and that find no default route
    Then each failing check is FAIL with its reason, the probe's exit code and at most eight lines of its log through redact_stream below it, or what the pod says of itself when the probe was killed, the other checks still pass, a missing or malformed line fails the checks it feeds, as do the lines of a net whose script did not end as it does, the run exits 1, and nothing printed holds an address or a control character

  Scenario: The runtime check fails with the reason when the class is unknown or not on the cluster, a pod does not start, or the pods do not end in time
    Given the same copy, whose recording kubectl has no RuntimeClass kata, a probe the kubelet refuses for its groups policy, a net pod that cannot pull its image or is not scheduled, pods deleted once they were made, Warning events for them, a probe that never ends, and kubectl errors that name an address
    When just runtime-check runs with docker as the class, an extra argument, an image without its digest and a bad timeout, and for each of those clusters
    Then the usage errors exit 2 before any kubectl call; a class not on the cluster fails every check naming it and creates nothing; a pod that does not start or end fails the checks it feeds with its status, scheduling condition, container state and events, without an address; a net that is not scheduled, or is deleted before it is, is the reason of every check and probe and reference are not created; a pod deleted once it ran fails the checks it feeds saying so; the pods still running when RUNTIME_CHECK_TIMEOUT passes are named; and every kubectl call is bounded by 30 seconds

  Scenario: The runtime check leaves nothing behind, however it ends, and clears what an earlier run left
    Given the same copy, whose recording kubectl holds an earlier run's namespace runtime-check with the managed-by label, one without it or with another manager's, a namespace another party makes between the run's read and its create, alone or with INT as the create is answered, a create of the namespace whose answer is lost or that the API server refuses, the run's namespace replaced by another party's labelled one while the run waits, as it reads its pods' logs or just before it makes net, or deleted by another party while the run waits or just before the run deletes it, a deletion of the run's whose answer is lost, and makes a run fail, fail a kubectl call, time out, or be interrupted by INT or TERM after the namespace was made, or keeps the namespace when it is deleted
    When just runtime-check runs in each case, and again after each
    Then an earlier run's namespace is deleted and waited for before anything is made, one without the label or with another manager's is refused with nothing changed, every run that made the namespace deletes it on its way out and waits until it is gone, by the uid it was made with or, when the create's answer was lost, by the label, a namespace another party made is neither deleted nor changed nor given a policy or pod and the run says so, a run makes no pod while another party's deletion of its namespace is under way and prints no check when its namespace was replaced as it read its pods' logs, a run whose namespace went without its own deletion answered as done exits 1, one whose namespace stays names it with the command that deletes it and exits 1, and the run after each runs whole

  Scenario: The runtime check's probe runs under the security context OpenShell gives a sandbox's workload, and the cluster's Pod Security, admission rules and network policy leave its pods alone
    Given the manifests the recording kubectl was given, the tree's default deny, the render of the tree and the tree's admission rules
    When the probe pod, the net and reference pods, the namespace and its two policies are read, Kyverno's podSecurity rule and both engines' rules are run over the pods and the namespace, and the flows from and to the pods are evaluated over the render with the namespace's objects added
    Then the probe pod is exactly OpenShell 0.1.9's workload context, its one container, its args and its one emptyDir at /tmp; net and reference are restricted, read-only, every capability dropped and no volume; each pod has its labels, no restart, the timeout as its deadline, no token, no service links, requests and a memory limit and nothing of the host; the namespace enforces restricted and carries the managed-by label, its default deny is the tree's and its policy lets net and reference reach the resolver on 53 over UDP and TCP and nothing else; every pod passes restricted with no entry in the exceptions list, both engines find nothing, net and reference reach the resolver and not the API server, the world, the host or another namespace's pod, probe reaches nothing, nothing reaches them, and the check of the render passes

  Scenario: The runtime check's images are pinned by digest where Renovate moves them: OpenShell's sandbox on its stable releases, the Cilium agents' image with its chart
    Given the justfile, the recipe, the script, the flow recorder's Deployment and .renovate/
    When the two image variables, the recipe that passes them and Renovate's configuration are read
    Then openshell_sandbox_image is OpenShell's sandbox image at 0.1.9 by its index digest, and another says what to check at the new release, and cilium_agent_image is the recorder's image, tag and digest, each under its comment and a renovate line that the justfile manager reads, and a mismatch says what to set; the script names no image; the tag forms, a rule that allows only stable releases of OpenShell's images, a group OpenShell and no automerge for them are in .renovate/, and the extraction record holds both pins

  Scenario: The docs say what the runtime check does and when to run it
    Given docs/kubernetes.md and docs/cluster.md
    When the section on runtime classes and the paragraph on the node image's runtimes are read
    Then one paragraph under Runtime classes opens with "Whether a runtime class can host a sandbox." and names just runtime-check, its four checks, the security context the probe runs under, OpenShell's support matrix and Cilium's Kata limitation at their pinned versions, the namespace and its policies, RUNTIME_CHECK_TIMEOUT and the lock, says it judges no class, and says to run it after every change of the node image and after a change of Cilium's values; and neither document says any longer that no workload selects a runtime class without naming the recipe
